# XTXT Documentation

Plain text that carries structure a machine can read without guessing — and
still opens in any editor and diffs cleanly in git.

This page is written in XTXT and rendered by `xtxt export`. Everything below
describes something the tool actually does; nothing here is planned or
aspirational.

## Install

Pick whichever fits how you work. The command line is the fastest way to see
what the format does.

@code(language="shell")
# Command line (Go)
go install github.com/SaiMouli3/xtxt/cmd/xtxt@latest

# JavaScript
npm install xtxt-js

# Python
pip install xtxt

# Rust
cargo add xtxt
@endcode

The VS Code extension is on the Marketplace as **XTXT** — search it in the
Extensions panel, or install from the command line:

@code(language="shell")
code --install-extension xtxt.xtxt
@endcode

## Your first document

Create `notes.xtxt`:

@code(language="text")
# Project Log

Neural networks are inspired by the brain, loosely.

@task
Title: Ship the reference parser
Status: In Progress
Owner: Subbu
@endtask
@endcode

Then look at it three ways:

@code(language="shell")
xtxt validate notes.xtxt    # is it well formed?
xtxt render   notes.xtxt    # read it in the terminal
xtxt extract  notes.xtxt    # what a machine sees
@endcode

That last command is the point of the format. `@task` is not a comment or a
naming convention — it is a block the parser returns, and `extract` hands it to
a program as JSON with the line it came from.

# Writing

## Text

Headings use `#` through `######`. Paragraphs are separated by a blank line.
Quotes begin with `>`.

@code(language="text")
# Title
## Section

A paragraph of prose.

> Something worth quoting.
@endcode

Inline marks: `*emphasis*`, `**strong**`, backticks for `code`, `~~strike~~`,
and `[text](url)` for links.

## Lists

@code(language="text")
- one
- two

1. first
2. second

- [ ] not done yet
- [x] done
@endcode

@note
Lists do not nest. An item indented deeper than the first is reported as a
warning rather than being silently flattened, so a formatting mistake cannot
quietly destroy your structure.
@endnote

## Directives

Everything that is not prose uses one syntax. `@name(args)` for a single item,
`@name … @endname` for a block.

@raw
@image(src="diagram.png", caption="How it fits together", width=600)

@code(language="python")
print("hello")
@endcode
@endraw

Arguments are `key=value` with quoted strings. The first positional argument
maps to the directive's main attribute, so `@image("photo.png")` and
`@image(src="photo.png")` mean the same thing.

## Media

@code(language="text")
@image(src="chart.png", alt="Signups by month", caption="Growth", width=600)
@video(src="demo.mp4", poster="thumb.png")
@audio(src="interview.mp3", caption="Field recording")
@attachment(src="report.pdf", name="Q3 Report")
@endcode

Sources may be relative paths or `data:` URIs. Pasting an image in VS Code or
running `xtxt paste` writes the file and inserts the directive for you.

## Tables

@code(language="text")
@table
Name | Age
-----|----
John | 20
@endtable
@endcode

The separator row is optional; without one the first row is the header. Colons
set alignment: `---:` right, `:---:` centre.

## Charts

A chart can be its own block:

@code(language="text")
@chart(type="bar", title="Monthly signups")
Jan | 20
Feb | 35
@endchart
@endcode

Or a table can draw itself, which keeps the numbers and the picture from ever
disagreeing:

@code(language="text")
@table(chart="bar", x="Month", y="Signups, Revenue")
Month | Signups | Revenue
------|---------|--------
Jan   | 20      | 1200
Feb   | 35      | 2400
@endtable
@endcode

Add a row and the chart follows. The table still renders underneath, so the
figures stay reachable as text.

Types are `bar`, `line`, `area`, `stacked` and `pie`. Charts render as inline
SVG with no script and no dependency, themed by CSS.

@table(chart="bar", x="Month", y="Signups", title="What that produces")
Month | Signups
------|--------
Jan   | 20
Feb   | 35
Mar   | 50
@endtable

## Records

A block of `Key: value` lines that a parser returns as data, sitting next to
the prose that explains it.

@code(language="text")
@decision
Title: Unknown directives are warnings, never errors
Why: A reader from today must stay useful on a document written tomorrow.
@enddecision
@endcode

Standard record types are `@task`, `@decision`, `@knowledge`, `@note`, `@ai`,
`@prompt` and `@chat`. Field names mean nothing to the format — it guarantees
the shape is preserved and reported, and what `Priority: High` means is your
application's business.

That is the same bargain HTML made with class names, and it is why
`@experiment` or `@invoice` works without anyone updating a parser.

## Code, maths and diagrams

@raw
@code(language="go", filename="main.go")
fmt.Println("hi")
@endcode

@math
E = mc^2
@endmath

@mermaid
graph LR; A-->B;
@endmermaid
@endraw

Pass `--mermaid` to `xtxt export` to load the diagram renderer from a CDN. It
is off by default so output stays self-contained and offline.

## Including other documents

@code(language="text")
@include(src="introduction.xtxt")
@embed(src="api-reference.xtxt")
@endcode

`@include` splices the target's blocks in place. `@embed` does the same but
demotes its headings by one level, so it nests under the including section
instead of competing with it. Use `--resolve` to expand them.

A resolver refuses absolute paths, anything escaping the including document's
directory, and remote sources unless the host opts in. It also bounds nesting
depth and total expansion, so a document cannot become a way to read arbitrary
files or exhaust memory.

## Metadata, comments and escaping

@code(language="text")
@metadata
title = Project Log
author = Subbu
@endmetadata

@comment
Never rendered. Notes to yourself live here.
@endcomment
@endcode

A backslash escapes any mark that would otherwise be read as syntax: `\*not
emphasis\*`.

# What a machine sees

## extract

`xtxt extract` returns everything an agent needs without inferring any of it
from prose.

@code(language="shell")
xtxt extract notes.xtxt
@endcode

@code(language="json")
{
  "outline": [{"level": 1, "text": "Project Log", "line": 1}],
  "tasks":   [{"title": "Ship the reference parser", "status": "In Progress",
               "owner": "Subbu", "done": false, "line": 5}],
  "blocks":  [{"type": "decision", "fields": {"title": "…", "why": "…"}}],
  "links": [], "media": [], "code": [], "words": 412
}
@endcode

Every entry carries a line number, so anything a program extracts can be
traced back to the source that produced it.

## ast

`xtxt ast` prints the parse tree itself — the same shape every implementation
is required to produce, and what the conformance suite pins.

# Command line

@table
Command | What it does
--------|-------------
`xtxt validate <file>` | Check syntax and report problems
`xtxt lint <file>` | Validate, plus style warnings
`xtxt render <file>` | Render to the terminal
`xtxt export <file> <format>` | Convert to html, body, md, text or json
`xtxt import <file.md>` | Convert Markdown to XTXT
`xtxt ast <file>` | Print the parse tree as JSON
`xtxt extract <file>` | Print the machine-facing view as JSON
`xtxt paste <file>` | Append the clipboard image to a document
@endtable

Useful flags:

- `--resolve` expands `@include` and `@embed` before rendering
- `--interactive` inlines the script that lets a reader read values off a
  chart and hide a series; without it, exported HTML stays script-free
- `--mermaid` loads the diagram renderer from a CDN
- `-o <path>` writes to a file instead of stdout
- `-w <n>` sets the wrap column for text output

Use `-` as the filename to read from standard input.

## Diagnostics

Validation reports two levels. An **error** means the file cannot be
interpreted unambiguously — an unclosed fence, an unclosed argument list. A
**warning** means it parses but is suspect — an unknown directive, a table row
with the wrong cell count.

`xtxt validate` exits non-zero only on errors. An unknown directive is never an
error, which is what lets a reader from today stay useful on a document written
tomorrow.

# Editors

## VS Code

Install **XTXT** from the Marketplace. It gives you:

- syntax highlighting, with `@code(language="…")` blocks highlighted in their
  own language
- a live preview that re-renders as you type, opened with `Cmd+K V`
- problems and squiggles in the editor and the Problems panel
- an outline covering headings **and** records, so `Cmd+Shift+O` jumps straight
  to a `@task` or `@decision`
- folding on blocks and headings
- image paste with `Cmd+V`, saved beside the document or embedded as a `data:`
  URI
- a `Chart this table` action that picks the type, the label column and the
  value columns

## Obsidian

The plugin renders XTXT documents and supports image paste.

# Libraries

@table
Language | Package | Parses | Renders
---------|---------|--------|--------
Go | `github.com/SaiMouli3/xtxt` | yes | yes
JavaScript | `xtxt-js` | yes | yes
Python | `xtxt` | yes | no
Rust | `xtxt` | yes | no
Java | `io.github.saimouli3:xtxt` | yes | no
C | `sdk/c` | yes | no
C++ | `sdk/cpp` | yes | no
@endtable

Every implementation is checked against the same conformance fixtures, which
pin the parse tree and the severity of every diagnostic — deliberately not the
wording, because implementations should agree on what is wrong, not on how to
phrase the complaint.

None of them has a runtime dependency.

## Using the library

@code(language="javascript")
import { parse, validate, extract, renderHTML } from 'xtxt-js';

const res = parse(source);
const issues = [...res.issues, ...validate(res.doc)];
const data = extract(res.doc);
const html = renderHTML(res.doc, { full: true });
@endcode

@code(language="python")
import xtxt

doc = xtxt.parse(source)
for issue in doc.issues:
    print(issue.severity, issue.line, issue.message)
@endcode

## MCP server

`xtxt-mcp` lets an agent read and write XTXT documents directly.

@code(language="shell")
npx xtxt-mcp
@endcode

# What XTXT does not do

Being clear about this is the point, not an apology.

@note
Title: Nothing nests
No nested lists, and no blocks inside blocks — so an admonition containing a
code sample is not expressible today. This keeps parsing to a single pass with
no backtracking, and it is the format's biggest limitation.
@endnote

@note
Title: Records have no schema yet
A record's field names are preserved and reported, but nothing declares that
Status is an enum or that Due is a date. Two authors can produce two
vocabularies.
@endnote

@note
Title: No compression, encryption, history or binary container
Each would trade away the property the format exists for — that the bytes on
disk are the document. These belong in a layer above: a file inside an
encrypted volume, a signature alongside it, history in the version control
that already does it well.
@endnote

